    "title": "California’s ‘Stop Nick Shirley Act’ draws concerns after fraud exposé",
    "text": "(NewsNation) — Conservative influencer Nick Shirley claims Democrats in the California State Legislature are trying to shut down anti‑fraud investigations after he posted videos alleging some publicly assisted hospices in the state are fraudulent. Shirley’s claims come as lawmakers consider a bill sponsored by Assemblymember Mia Bonta, D‑Alameda, that would prohibit others from posting certain information about providers of immigration‑related services. Bonta has said the measure is intended to protect providers amid a tense political climate. Critics argue the bill could limit the ability of citizen journalists or watchdogs to expose fraud. Shirley rose to prominence late last year after posting a viral video alleging Somali Americans in Minnesota were collecting taxpayer money through fraudulent or nonexistent child care centers. His supporters have dubbed the California proposal the “Stop Nick Shirley Act.” California lawmakers defend proposal as protections for immigrant providers “This is all about bullying and bankrupting citizen journalists,” California Assemblymember Carl DeMaio, a Republican from San Diego, told Shirley in a video posted Saturday. Bonta has defended the measure, saying it is designed to prevent harassment or violence against immigrant service providers and would not bar journalists from asking legitimate questions. California Rep. Kevin Kiley, an independent who opposes the bill, said existing laws already protect individuals from harassment. “It’s very telling: They’re proactively trying not to stop the fraud itself but to stop those who are exposing the fraud,” Kiley told “NewsNation Prime.” Nick Shirley alleges widespread hospice fraud In a recent exposé, Shirley said hospice enrollment in California has increased by about 1,000% in recent years. He claims to have identified $170 million in suspicious billings and alleged some hospice operators were driving luxury vehicles. In response, the press office of Gov. Gavin Newsom, a Democrat, criticized Shirley’s reporting. Critics of the bill, however, argue that policymakers focused on reform should welcome information about potential government waste, regardless of the source.",
